0 #edb/ref 1080 "0.50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089530 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089530 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "de8f08a7-ee40-5001-9780-39a8ddcc4bed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091925 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10109" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091925 #edb/ref 1071 "A store has $40$ employees, of which $21$ are female. The store is divided into three departments, $A, B,$ and $C.$ There are $7$ female workers in department $A,$ while there are $8$ female workers in department $C.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected store worker is a female that works in department $B?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091925 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c690991e-6029-5067-b97a-67c5e62b7f47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091925 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that the chosen worker is a female, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected worker works department $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected worker works department $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected worker works department $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A \\sqcup B \\sqcup C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nRecall that the law of total probability states that\n\n$$\nP(F) = P(F \\cap A) + P(F \\cap B) + P(F \\cap C).\n$$\n\nWe wish to find $P(F \\cap B)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- To calculate $P(F \\cap A),$ we take the number of females who work in department $A$ $(7)$ and divide by the total number of workers $(40).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(F \\cap A) = \\dfrac{7}{40}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(F \\cap C),$ we take the number of females who work in department $C$ $(8)$ and divide by the total number of workers $(40).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(F \\cap C) = \\dfrac{8}{40}.\n$$\n\n- Also, we're given that\n\n$$\nP(F) = \\dfrac{21}{40}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(F \\cap B),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F) & =P(F\\cap A)+P(F\\cap B)+P(F\\cap C) \\\\ \\frac{21}{40} & =\\frac{7}{40}+P(F\\cap B)+\\frac{8}{40} \\\\ P(F\\cap B) & =\\frac{21}{40}-\\frac{7}{40}-\\frac{8}{40}. \\\\ P(F\\cap B) & =\\frac{6}{40} \\\\ P(F\\cap B) & =\\frac{3}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091926 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10110" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091926 #edb/ref 1071 "Three factories produce the same spare part for a particular car model. Factory ${A}$ produces $32\\%$ of the overall parts, of which $1.5\\%$ are defective. Factory ${B}$ produces $33\\%$ of the overall parts, of which $2\\%$ are defective, and Factory ${C}$ produces $35\\%$ of the overall parts, of which $1.4\\%$ are defective. If one car part is selected randomly, what is the probability that it is defective?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091926 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1104f073-7f90-51ce-9e95-aae0935ca852" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091926 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly selected part is defective, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected part was produced by Factory ${A},$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected part was produced by Factory ${B},$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected part was produced by Factory ${C}.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A \\sqcup B \\sqcup C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected part is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since Factory ${A}$ produces $32\\%$ of the parts, Factory ${B}$ produces $33 \\%$ of the parts, and Factory ${C}$ produces $35 \\%$ of the parts, we have\n\n$$\nP(A) = 0.32, \\qquad P(B) = 0.33, \\qquad P(C)=0.35.\n$$\n\n- Since $1.5\\%$ of the parts produced by Factory ${A},$ $2\\%$ of the parts produced by Factory ${B},$ and $1.4\\%$ of the parts produced by Factory ${C}$ are defective, we have \n\n$$\nP(D | A) = 0.015, \\qquad P(D | B) = 0.02, \\qquad P(D | C) = 0.014.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected part is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(0.015)(0.32)+(0.02)(0.33)+(0.014)(0.35) \\\\  & =0.004\\,8+0.006\\,6+0.004\\,9 \\\\  & =0.016\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091927 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10111" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091927 #edb/ref 1071 "Three baskets, $A, B,$ and $C,$ contain apples. It is known that $\\dfrac25$ of the apples in basket $A$ and $\\dfrac14$ of the apples in basket $B$ are red. A basket is selected at random, and an apple is randomly chosen from the basket. If the probability that the selected apple is red is $\\dfrac13,$ what proportion of the apples from basket $C$ are red?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091927 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae39bb45-ac10-562a-bac6-4f192c5f0aed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091927 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $R$ be the event that the selected apple is red, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected apple is from basket ${A},$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected apple is from basket ${B},$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected apple is from basket ${C}.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A \\sqcup B \\sqcup C$ covers the entire sample space. \n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a red apple, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap B)+P(R\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since a basket is randomly selected and there are $3$ baskets, we have\n\n$$\nP(A) = P(B) = P(C) = \\dfrac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\dfrac25$ of the apples in basket $A$ and $\\dfrac14$ of the apples in basket $B$ are red, we have\n\n$$\nP(R | A)= \\dfrac25, \\qquad P(R | B) = \\dfrac14.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability of picking a red apple from any of the three baskets is $\\dfrac13,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(R) = \\dfrac13.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(R|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =(\\frac{2}{5})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{4})(\\frac{1}{3})+P(R|C)(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{2}{15}+\\frac{1}{12}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{15}-\\frac{1}{12} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) & =\\frac{7}{60} \\\\ P(R|C) & =\\frac{7}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\dfrac{7}{20}$ of the apples from basket $C$ are red." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092314 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092314 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092314 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092314 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092314 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092314 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092314 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092314 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092314 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092314 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092314 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092314 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying the Law of Total Probability" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092314 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186091925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092314 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4149b2fe-4243-595e-af7f-851b308f7eb1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092315 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092315 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092315 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092315 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092315 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092315 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092315 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092315 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092315 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092315 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092315 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying the Law of Total Probability in Terms of Conditional Probability" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092315 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186091926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092315 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "96f54294-8bb4-5338-969b-76c8d1536daa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092316 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092316 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092316 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092316 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092316 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092316 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092316 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092316 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092316 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092316 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186515560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092316 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Conditional Probability Using the Law of Total Probability" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092316 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186091927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092316 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f0ebe700-63af-57d4-ae8f-9e09dac5cf76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092453 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092453 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092453 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092453 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092453 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092453 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092453 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092453 #edb/ref 1045 "Extending the Law of Total Probability" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092453 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186093201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092453 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092453 #edb/ref 1050 840.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092453 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "0c0d6ec4-01bc-5b0f-8b44-162a9c686020" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092558 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092558 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143529" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092558 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092558 #edb/ref 1071 "A company manufactures electronic components. Of the components manufactured, $45%$ are produced by the first machine, $25%$ are produced by the second machine, and the third machine produces the remaining.\nIf $8%$ of the components produced by the first machine, $4%$ produced by the second machine, and $5%$ produced by the third machine are defective, what is the probability that a randomly selected component is defective?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092558 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f910c2f8-2684-5671-bb61-e8e8d6c2b119" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092558 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092558 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly selected component is defective, and \n\n- let ${B}_{1}$ be the event that the component was produced by the first machine,\n\n- let ${B}_{2}$ be the event that the component was produced by the second machine,\n\n- let ${B}_{3}$ be the event that the component was produced by the third machine.\n\nThen the disjoint union ${B}_{1}⊔{B}_{2}⊔{B}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap {B}_{1})+P(D\\cap {B}_{2})+P(D\\cap {B}_{3}) \\\\  & =P(D|{B}_{1})P({B}_{1})+P(D|{B}_{2})P({B}_{2})+P(D|{B}_{3})P({B}_{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since the first machine produces $45%$ of the components, the second machine produces $25%$ of the components, the third machine produces the remaining\n\n$$\n100%-(45%+25%)=30%.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nP({B}_{1})=0.45,\\,P({B}_{2})=0.25,\\,P({B}_{3})=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $8%$ of the components produced by the first machine, $4%$ produced by the second machine, and $5%$ produced by the third machine are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|{B}_{1})=0.08,\\,P(D|{B}_{2})=0.04,\\,P(D|{B}_{3})=0.05.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected component is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|{B}_{1})P({B}_{1})+P(D|{B}_{2})P({B}_{2})+P(D|{B}_{3})P({B}_{3}) \\\\  & =(0.08)(0.45)+(0.04)(0.25)+(0.05)(0.3) \\\\  & =0.036+0.01+0.015 \\\\  & =0.061.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093201 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093201 #edb/ref 1061 836329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093201 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "34e9e254-fd36-5512-88cc-6abf808e561b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093201 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093202 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093202 #edb/ref 1061 836331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093202 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "92a781b8-11f6-5279-b328-89be09bb0155" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093202 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093203 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093203 #edb/ref 1061 836334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093203 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a17de8a8-a153-5e7d-be7b-53093b1da395" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093203 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093204 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093204 #edb/ref 1061 836335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093204 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "80843a9c-ed90-5e4d-9462-e0eb6c40068c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093204 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093205 #edb/ref 1061 836338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093205 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b7b7a4f5-8ab3-527e-b01f-844c9aeadcbe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093205 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093206 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093206 #edb/ref 1061 836330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093206 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1477f9c6-f997-5b76-9d95-747c31eb50a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093206 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093475 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that three events, $B_1, B_2,$ and $B_3,$ exist within a sample space $\\mathcal S$ such that \n\n- $B_1, B_2,$ and $B_3$ are *mutually exclusive*, and\n\n- the events $B_1, B_2,$ and $B_3$ cover the *entire* sample space.\n\nWhen these two conditions are satisfied, we say that $\\mathcal S$ is the **disjoint union** of  $B_1, B_2,$ and $B_3,$ and we write \n \n\n$$\n\\mathcal S = B_1 \\sqcup B_2 \\sqcup B_3.\n$$\n\nA diagram of this situation is shown below. Notice that $B_1, B_2,$ and $B_3$ cover the entire diagram but do not overlap.\n\n![](images/f5/f5f9f04eb6affb9dca1f7d4e7d66d76c4d520cbf66db93db60639ce896fb19af.png)\n\n \n\nNow suppose that we wish to compute the probability of an event $A$ that lies within $\\mathcal S.$ Let's draw this event on our diagram and label its intersections with $B_1, B_2, B_3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/ed/ed6568369a3af39cf1f6ecd5cb481de20d6c31bbce0fa1be54eb9ae0ab4e7f20.png)\n\n \n\nThe **law of total probability** can be deduced from this diagram. It states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =P(A\\cap {B}_{1})+P(A\\cap {B}_{2})+P(A\\cap {B}_{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we will only consider a disjoint union of $\\mathcal S$ consisting of three events. However, it should be noted that the law of total probability can be extended to an arbitrary number of events, that is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =P(A\\cap {B}_{1})+P(A\\cap {B}_{2})+⋯ \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i}P(A\\cap {B}_{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathcal S = B_1 \\sqcup B_2\\sqcup \\ldots$ is a disjoint union of $\\mathcal S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093475 #edb/ref 1065 6295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093475 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d4103a1f-00a1-55d2-8e99-f1b230f8b348" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093475 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093476 #edb/ref 1064 "The law of total probability states that\n\n$$\nP(A) = P(A\\cap B_1) +  P(A\\cap B_2) +  P(A\\cap B_3),\n$$\n\nwhere $B_1\\sqcup B_2\\sqcup B_3$ is a disjoint union of the sample space $\\mathcal S.$\n\nOften, it's more helpful to express this law using conditional probabilities. \n\nFrom the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cap {B}_{1}) & =P(A|{B}_{1})P({B}_{1}) \\\\ P(A\\cap {B}_{2}) & =P(A|{B}_{2})P({B}_{2}) \\\\ P(A\\cap {B}_{3}) & =P(A|{B}_{3})P({B}_{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above form into the law of total probability, we get\n\n$$\nP(A) = P(A|B_1)P(B_1)+  P(A|B_2)P(B_2) +  P(A|B_3)P(B_3).\n$$\n\nLet's see an example of how to apply this." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093476 #edb/ref 1065 6296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093476 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "344e2752-b37e-517e-8057-b24f42e51566" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093476 #edb/ref 1067 "The Law of Total Probability in Terms of Conditional Probability" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093477 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that a particular garden is divided into three small plots. There are $10$ roses in the first plot, of which $5$ are red, $12$ roses in the second plot, of which $6$ are red, and $14$ roses in the third plot, of which $4$ are red. What is the probability that a randomly selected rose from the garden is red?\n\nLet $A$ be the event that a chosen rose is red, and \n\n- let $B_1$ be the event that the selected rose comes from the first plot,\n\n- let $B_2$ be the event that the selected rose comes from the second plot,\n\n- let $B_3$ be the event that the selected rose comes from the third plot.\n\nNotice that the disjoint union\n\n$$\nB_1\\sqcup B_2\\sqcup B_3\n$$\n\ncovers the entire sample space, and that there are a total of \n\n$$\n10+12+14=36\n$$\n\nroses in the garden. \n\n- To calculate $P(A \\cap B_1),$ we take the number of red roses in the first plot $(5)$ and divide by the total number of roses $(36).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(A \\cap B_1) = \\dfrac{5}{36}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(A \\cap B_2),$ we take the number of red roses in the second plot $(6)$ and divide by the total number of roses $(36).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(A \\cap B_2) = \\dfrac{6}{36}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(A \\cap B_3),$ we take the number of red roses in the third plot $(4)$ and divide by the total number of roses $(36).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(A \\cap B_3) = \\dfrac{4}{36}.\n$$\n\nSo, to calculate the probability that a randomly chosen rose is red, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =P(A\\cap {B}_{1})+P(A\\cap {B}_{2})+P(A\\cap {B}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{36}+\\frac{6}{36}+\\frac{4}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNote the following: \n\n- You may have noticed that there is an easier way to solve this problem. That is, to sum the total number of red roses $(5+6+4 = 15)$ and divide by the total number of roses $(10+12+14 = 36).$ However, you're encouraged to work with the law of total probability as it's essential to get used to the concept.\n\n- A little later in this lesson, we'll work with a version of the law of total probability that isn't intuitive. Understanding the law of total probability in its simplest form will help when things get trickier.\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093477 #edb/ref 1065 6446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093477 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9b6238d0-0917-5d62-b878-311127fce7fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093477 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436144 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436144 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436144 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "05abde05-5ea0-5346-b70b-e15ddb38a2b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436145 #edb/ref 1080 "0.27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436145 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436145 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0af764f3-b723-5507-8469-f93dbd86be3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436147 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{6}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436147 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436147 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "18474b78-45ed-57a3-8328-b554a713c479" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436148 #edb/ref 1080 "0.55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436148 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436148 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid 